/* book exam traps: Marking a photodiode passive by habit. It is active in photovoltaic mode (no bias, light makes the emf) and passive in photoconductive mode (reverse bias, light changes the current). | Confusing the sensing element with the transducer. The diaphragm is the sensing element; the capacitive pressure transducer is the whole device with its terminals. | Calling a passive sensor “no power needed”. It makes no signal of its own and reads zero without excitation; it draws current from that supply, which is why the excitation level matters. | Writing 'active sensor' for any sensor that needs a power supply to run its amplifier. The label describes the sensing element only. */
